The `{{ .Container }}` extension of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: a VS
Code extension in TypeScript, bundled with esbuild, scaffolded by theos.

## Use it

Install the `.vsix` from a release: in VS Code, run **Extensions: Install from
VSIX...**, or `code --install-extension {{ .Container }}-<version>.vsix`. Then run
**{{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}: Say Hello** from the Command Palette.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24 and the Go toolchain.

```sh
npm ci               # install the exact tools in package-lock.json
npm run build        # bundle src/ into out/extension.js
npm test             # unit tests with vitest; no VS Code needed
{{- if .Standard }}
make check           # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, package
make dist            # dist/{{ .Container }}-<version>.vsix
make help            # every target
{{- else }}
npm run package      # the .vsix
{{- end }}
```

## Layout

- `src/extension.ts` activates the extension: it registers commands and
  providers and puts every registration in `context.subscriptions`.
- `src/*.ts` are the extension's modules. Keep logic in modules that don't import
  `vscode`, so plain unit tests cover it; tests sit next to them as `*.test.ts`.
- `test/vscode.ts` stands in for the `vscode` module in unit tests
  (`vitest.config.mts` points `vscode` at it).
- `esbuild.mjs` bundles into `out/`; `.vscodeignore` keeps everything else out of
  the `.vsix`.
